<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.WebAssembly">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-browser</TargetFramework>
    <RuntimeIdentifier>browser-wasm</RuntimeIdentifier>
    <RootNamespace>Detangle.Browser</RootNamespace>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <WasmMainJSPath>wwwroot\main.js</WasmMainJSPath>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CA1416</NoWarn>

    <!--
      No .br and no .gz siblings. The demo is served from GitHub Pages, which compresses
      on the fly and does not negotiate against sibling files, and dotnet.js never asks
      for one either — grep the published loader for ".br", ".gz", "brotli" or
      "content-encoding" and there is nothing to find. Publishing them added 22,360,259
      bytes to the site (59,613,397 -> 37,253,138) and 22,430,154 bytes to every Pages
      artifact upload, for files no visitor ever fetched. It changes nothing a visitor
      downloads; it is deploy weight, not page weight.

      If the site ever moves to a host that does serve precompressed siblings, this is
      the wrong switch and the pair below is the right one:
        <EnableDefaultCompressionFormats>false</EnableDefaultCompressionFormats>
        <PublishCompressionFormats>brotli</PublishCompressionFormats>
      Both are needed together: while the default flag is on, the SDK appends
      ";gzip;brotli" to PublishCompressionFormats and setting the format alone yields
      "brotli;gzip;brotli".
    -->
    <CompressionEnabled>false</CompressionEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Trimming. Unlike the desktop head, none of this turns trimming on: the WebAssembly SDK
    already publishes a browser-wasm head with PublishTrimmed=true and TrimMode=full, which
    is stricter than the desktop's "partial". Measured on this project, publishing with
    -p:PublishTrimmed=false costs 8,315,793 gzip bytes of first paint (12,055,121 ->
    20,370,914) and ships 140 assemblies that are otherwise gone. The properties are
    written out so that a future SDK default cannot switch them off silently, and so this
    file says what the demo depends on rather than inheriting it invisibly.

    Deliberately not set here:
    - TrimMode=partial. Copying the desktop head's value would make the demo bigger.
    - ILLinkTreatWarningsAsErrors=false. The desktop head needs it because YamlDotNet and
      Avalonia.DesignerSupport report there. This head reports nothing, so a new trim
      warning should stop the build rather than ship a demo that renders blank.
    - InvariantGlobalization. It looks like the largest remaining wasm knob and it is a
      trap: with it on, an NFD "e" + U+0301 pair passed to String.Normalize(FormC) comes
      back unchanged and IsNormalized() answers true — measured on net10.0, no exception
      anywhere. The resolver's NFC fold in LinkNormalizer and HeadingSlugger would then
      never match an NFD filename, which is exactly the silent failure Directory.Build.props
      refuses. It would have bought 220,093 bytes, the one ICU shard a visitor downloads.
    - TrimmerRootAssembly. None is needed: a full-trim publish with TrimmerSingleWarn=false
      and SuppressTrimAnalysisWarnings=false emits no IL2xxx or IL3xxx at all.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Release'">
    <PublishTrimmed>true</PublishTrimmed>
    <TrimMode>full</TrimMode>

    <!-- Every trim warning at its own site, the way the desktop head reports them, so a
         newly trim-hostile dependency is visible instead of aggregated into one line. -->
    <TrimmerSingleWarn>false</TrimmerSingleWarn>
    <SuppressTrimAnalysisWarnings>false</SuppressTrimAnalysisWarnings>

    <!--
      The one measured saving in this group, and it is not the trimmer's.
      WasmNativeDebugSymbols defaults to true even in Release, which passes -g to emcc: it
      keeps 10,703 extra functions alive in dotnet.native.wasm (32,272 -> 21,569) and stops
      the emscripten JS minifier running at all. Turning it off is worth 85,572 gzip bytes
      of first paint; dotnet.native.wasm loses 408,074 raw bytes and dotnet.native.js
      another 188,931, while every managed asset stays byte-identical. What is given up is
      native function names in a browser stack trace, on a page with no debugger attached;
      one publish with -p:WasmNativeDebugSymbols=true brings them back when that is wanted.

      Do not reach for -p:EmccLinkOptimizationFlag=-Oz alongside it. Measured: it shrinks
      dotnet.native.wasm by 68,974 raw bytes and the result compresses so much worse that
      first paint goes UP by 96,098.
    -->
    <WasmNativeDebugSymbols>false</WasmNativeDebugSymbols>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Browser" />
    <PackageReference Include="SkiaSharp.NativeAssets.WebAssembly" />
    <PackageReference Include="HarfBuzzSharp.NativeAssets.WebAssembly" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Detangle.App\Detangle.App.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Detangle.Rendering\Detangle.Rendering.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The demo wiki is compiled into the assembly rather than fetched. A browser has no
    filesystem to point the scanner at, and fetching a folder of markdown would mean a
    network call on a page whose whole claim is that the app does not make any.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\..\samples\**\*.md" LinkBase="Samples" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
